OiNutter wrote:
> I've installed Nethack in my home directory on a Fedora system about a 
> week ago.  It was the latest binary version from nethack.org so all I 
> changed was the variable for pointing to the playground.  However, 
> friends on the network were having a hard time using my playground 
> unless I changed permissions on the playground to allow writing by 
> others (chmod -R 0777).
> 
> Now that we're getting more people to play, I don't want the scoreboard 
> and records to be open to deletion by others, so how can I change the 
> application's UID so that it writes to files using my name instead of 
> trying to access them with the player's uid?  Keep in mind that I'm not 
> the system/network administrator.

Making the binary setuid should solve your problems (so regardless of 
who runs it, it'll run with its owner's (i.e. your) permissions.

However, as a general comment before setuid-ing anything, be aware that 
it's a reasonable amount of power you're handing over. I don't profess 
to be very aware of what Nethack lets you do, but for example if it were 
to offer the ability to open a shell, that would obviously then be A Bad 
Thing (tm). It'd definitely be worth considering disabling the mail 
stuff (again, I don't know details of how it works, never looked at 
nethack source, nor used the mail code, thus the emphasis on "considering"

